Let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^2$ be a bounded convex domain with smooth boundary $\partial\Omega$, and let $H \in \mathbb{R}\setminus\{0\}$ be a constant. Denote by $|\Omega|$ the area of $\Omega$, by $L$ the length of $\partial\Omega$, and by $\operatorname{diam}(\Omega)$ its diameter. Consider the Dirichlet problem for the constant mean curvature equation
\[
\operatorname{div}\!\left(\frac{Du}{\sqrt{1+|Du|^2}}\right) = 2H \quad \text{in } \Omega, \qquad u = 0 \quad \text{on } \partial\Omega,
\]
which seeks $u \in C^2(\Omega)\cap C^0(\overline{\Omega})$ whose graph is a surface of constant mean curvature $H$ spanning $\partial\Omega$. Determine whether this Dirichlet problem admits a (classical) solution under each of the following geometric hypotheses, taken separately:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $|\Omega|\, H^2 < \pi$;
\item $L\, H^2 < 2\pi$;
\item $\operatorname{diam}(\Omega) < 2/|H|$.
\end{enumerate}
In each case, either prove existence of a solution or exhibit a convex domain $\Omega$ satisfying the hypothesis for which no solution exists.

